The original language release and untouched Base completed zero of five diagnostic episodes. We trained rank-32 adapters on the final attention and MLP projections of native layer 23 and repeated that layer twice with a fixed residual coefficient of 0.35. The frozen prefix has its original parameters. We cached prefix hidden states for supervised training; at generation the decoder processes the entire request and each repeated site maintains its own cache. Both arms receive an open thinking turn. Free greedy decoding determines when to close it, which function to call, and which JSON arguments to supply.

The initial curriculum contains 180 training episodes over six private table domains. Their calls and observations yield 360 supervised turns. AdamW used 450 updates, minibatches of four and a learning rate of 0.0003. All 802,816 trainable parameters were present in both the two-pass and one-pass fits. The full-vocabulary cross-entropy loss covers every response token including the chat turn boundary. The fit used 19,364 response target tokens over sampled batches. The independently repeated fit produced the same adapter SHA-256 after an initial metadata-write failure.

The evaluator constructs only the catalog, question and tool observations for the model. Live Python callbacks perform lookups into request-specific data. A correct end-to-end score on lookup, chaining or comparison requires the exact call path and a correct final answer. The comparison allows either order of the two reads. A direct answer or clarification must avoid all calls. A clarification must mention the relevant entity type and ask a question. Each model receives the same initial prompt IDs, tools, order, per-turn budget and maximum of four turns. Once models take different actions, later transcripts necessarily differ. Wall time includes callback execution and all model turns; model loading and fitting are outside these timers.

The first implementation stopped only at document end. It treated a legitimate chat terminator as extra text after JSON. We preserved the resulting 6/30 tie. We then registered a fresh 30-episode comparison with identical frozen weights and corrected termination in both arms. The recurrent candidate completed 20/30 versus 5/30 in 13.14 vs 51.56 seconds. There were 15 wins and zero losses (two-sided exact paired $p = 0.000061$). Peak MLX allocation was 1.67 versus 1.63 GiB. Both arms had 128 output tokens per turn. The model's weakest family was comparing the values from two calls.

#figure(image("tools.svg",width: 94%),caption: [Fresh corrected-executor successes by task. Each family contributes six episodes. Correct arguments without a supported final answer earn no end-to-end credit.])

Under identical exposure, the one-pass model completed 19/30 in 10.88 seconds against 20/30 in 12.93 seconds for two passes; the paired comparison had two wins and one loss ($p = 1$). Optimizer time was 39.47 seconds for two passes versus 25.11 seconds for one. The large improvement over untouched Base includes training to use the message protocol. This comparison cannot attribute it to the second pass.

The four held-out domains contributed net gains of five (printing), three (observatory), two (market) and five (festival). A sensitivity analysis that flips the sign jointly for every episode in a domain gives two-sided $p = 0.125$. Four domains give little power for inference to a population of new schemas even though the episode-level registered gate passed.
